<h1>Orcraft Configuration Fields</h1>
<p>Config fields are how a component declares what can be configured about it. They are plain class
  attributes, and they are read by the controller to build its run configuration panel, to validate a run
  before it starts, and to render the config table in the log.</p>

<h2 id="declaring">Declaring a field</h2>
<p>Config fields come from the <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> namespace and are declared on a config
  class:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyTestConfig(OrcraftTestConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Test")

	app_folder = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFolder(
		default="",
		label="App Folder",
		required=True,
		layout=OrcraftConfigField.Layout.NewRow,
	)

	attempts = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=3, label="Attempts")') }}

<p>Inside the component the field reads as an ordinary attribute -
  <code>self.config.app_folder</code> - holding either the configured value or the declared
  default. There is no lookup by string key and no dictionary to keep in sync.</p>

<p>Output fields work the same way, from the <code>OrcraftResultField</code> namespace - see
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/results-and-statuses/">Orcraft Results and Statuses</a>.</p>

<h2 id="options">Options every field accepts</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Option</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>default</code></td>
      <td>The value used when nothing else is configured.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>default_factory</code></td>
      <td>A callable producing the default. Use this - never <code>default</code> - for lists and for anything that constructs objects, so instances are not shared. Only one of the two may be given.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>label</code></td>
      <td>The name shown in the GUI and in log tables. Without it the field is not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>required</code></td>
      <td>Blocks a run that leaves the field empty, reporting it as a missing argument. Numeric fields are required by default; pass <code>required=False</code> for one that may legitimately be blank.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>layout</code></td>
      <td>Where the field is placed in the GUI panel - see <a class="text-accent" href="#layout">Layout and presentation</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>width</code></td>
      <td>Width hint, in grid units, for the input in the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>formatter</code></td>
      <td>A callable that renders the value for display, leaving the stored value untouched.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>input_placeholder</code></td>
      <td>Placeholder text shown in an empty input.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>button_text</code></td>
      <td>Caption for the field's action button, where it has one.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>options</code></td>
      <td>The fixed set of values a selection field offers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>options_lambda</code></td>
      <td>A callable evaluated when the panel opens, for a selection whose values are not known in advance.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>endpoints</code></td>
      <td>For package fields: which endpoint fields this file or folder should be uploaded to.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="basic-types">Basic types</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Notes</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Text</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>Free text. Non-string values are coerced with <code>str()</code>; <code>None</code> becomes an empty string.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Numeric</code></td>
      <td><code>int</code> or <code>float</code></td>
      <td>Required by default, because a cleared numeric input has nothing to fall back on.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Boolean</code></td>
      <td><code>bool</code></td>
      <td>Rendered as a checkbox.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{ codeblock.block('name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Boolean Parameter")
verbose = OrcraftConfigField.Boolean(default=False, label="Verbose")
attempts = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=3, label="Attempts")
timeout = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=None, label="Timeout", required=False)') }}

<h2 id="selection-types">Selection types</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Notes</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Select</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>One value from a fixed <code>options</code> tuple.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>MultiSelect</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>Any number of values from a fixed <code>options</code> tuple.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>SelectLambda</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>One value from a list produced by <code>options_lambda</code> at configuration time.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>MultiSelectLambda</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>Any number of values from a list produced by <code>options_lambda</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Use the lambda variants when the choices are not knowable when the test is written - a list of
  firmware versions on a server, branches in a repository, devices currently attached. The callable runs
  when the run configuration is opened, so the list is current rather than committed to source.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('mode = OrcraftConfigField.Select(
	default="fast",
	label="Mode",
	options=("fast", "thorough"),
)

version = OrcraftConfigField.SelectLambda(
	default="",
	label="Version",
	options_lambda=list_available_versions,
)') }}

<h2 id="filesystem-types">Filesystem types</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Selects</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>File</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>A single file path.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Folder</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>A single folder path.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>FileList</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>Any number of file paths.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>FolderList</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>Any number of folder paths.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>These give the GUI a browse button and store the chosen path. The path is used as-is: the file stays
  where it is, and the test reads it from there.</p>

<h2 id="package-types">Package upload types</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Selects</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>PackageFile</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>A single file, uploaded with the test package.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>PackageFolder</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>A single folder, uploaded with the test package.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>PackageFileList</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>Any number of files, uploaded with the test package.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>PackageFolderList</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>Any number of folders, uploaded with the test package.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>A package field is a filesystem field that travels. The controller uploads what it points at to the
  endpoints that need it before the run, and inside the test the value resolves to the path on the
  machine the code is executing on. That is the difference that matters: a <code>Folder</code> is a path
  on the controller, a <code>PackageFolder</code> is a path that exists wherever the step runs.</p>

<p>The <code>endpoints</code> option binds a package field to specific endpoint fields, so files upload
  only where they are actually needed:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Test Endpoint")
aux_endpoints = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectEndpoints(default=("localhost",), label="Aux Endpoints")

input_file = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFile(
	default="",
	label="Input File",
	required=True,
	endpoints=[test_endpoint],
)

input_folders = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFolderList(
	default=(),
	label="Input Folders",
	required=True,
	endpoints=[test_endpoint, aux_endpoints],
)') }}

<p>Without <code>endpoints</code>, a package field is uploaded to every endpoint the test uses.</p>

<h2 id="endpoint-types">Endpoint types</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Endpoint</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>One endpoint the test uses. A test may declare several - a producer and a consumer, for instance.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>A set of endpoints used together <em>within one run</em>, iterated by the test itself.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>EndpointFanout</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>A set of endpoints that each get <em>their own run</em>. Selecting three endpoints spawns three test runs.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>All three accept <code>port</code>, <code>secure_enabled</code> and <code>shared</code> in addition to
  the shared options. <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/">Orcraft Endpoints</a> covers what they
  do and when to reach for each field type.</p>

<h2 id="composition-types">Composition types</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Declared on</th>
      <th>Holds</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Groups</code></td>
      <td><a class="text-accent" href="/framework/suites/">Orcraft Test Suite</a> config</td>
      <td>The group instances the suite runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Tests</code></td>
      <td><a class="text-accent" href="/framework/groups/">Orcraft Test Group</a> config</td>
      <td>The test instances the group runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Matrix</code></td>
      <td><a class="text-accent" href="/framework/test/">Orcraft Test</a> config</td>
      <td>The dimensions that expand into cases.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Dimension</code></td>
      <td>Inside a matrix</td>
      <td>One named axis and its values.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><code>Groups</code> and <code>Tests</code> always use <code>default_factory</code>, because they
  construct component instances:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('tests = OrcraftConfigField.Tests(
	default_factory=lambda: [
		MyFirstTest(),
		MySecondTest(),
	]
)') }}

<p><code>Matrix</code> and <code>Dimension</code> have options of their own - generated values, exclusion
  rules, randomization and seeding. They are covered in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/">Orcraft Matrix</a>, alongside what they
  produce.</p>

<h2 id="layout">Layout and presentation</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Member</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Layout.NewRow</code></td>
      <td>Start the field on a new row of the configuration panel.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Layout.UnderPrevious</code></td>
      <td>Place the field directly beneath the previous one.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Format</code></td>
      <td>A presentation-only field, used for settings such as log orientation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>LogTableOrientation.HORIZONTAL</code></td>
      <td>Log the config table as a row of columns - compact for a handful of fields.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL</code></td>
      <td>Log one field per line - readable for many fields or long values.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{ codeblock.block('log_orientation = OrcraftConfigField.Format(
	default=OrcraftConfigField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL
)') }}

<h2 id="built-in-fields">Fields you inherit</h2>
<p>Every config class starts with fields it did not declare. Overriding one in your own config replaces
  its default without changing what it does.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Declared on</th>
      <th>Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td>Every component</td>
      <td>Display name in the GUI, CLI and logs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td>Every component</td>
      <td>Free text describing what the component does.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td>Every component</td>
      <td>Orientation of the logged config and result tables.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>version</code></td>
      <td>Suite, group, test</td>
      <td>Version recorded with the results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>parallel</code></td>
      <td>Suite, group</td>
      <td>Whether children may run concurrently.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>groups</code> / <code>tests</code></td>
      <td>Suite / group</td>
      <td>The children the component runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>test_endpoint</code></td>
      <td>Test</td>
      <td>The endpoint the test executes against.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>matrix</code></td>
      <td>Test</td>
      <td>The dimensions that expand into cases.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>number_of_cycles</code></td>
      <td>Test</td>
      <td>How many times the whole test repeats.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>case_number_of_cycles</code></td>
      <td>Test</td>
      <td>How many times each case repeats.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>upload_test_package</code></td>
      <td>Suite, group, test</td>
      <td>Whether the test package is uploaded to the endpoint before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>update_endpoint_app</code></td>
      <td>Suite, group, test</td>
      <td>Whether the endpoint application is updated before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>target_endpoint</code></td>
      <td>Step</td>
      <td>The endpoint the step is dispatched to, set by the flow that runs it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>expect_endpoint_disconnect</code></td>
      <td>Step</td>
      <td>Marks a step that is meant to take its endpoint offline.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Not every inherited field is user-facing. <code>name</code> and <code>description</code>, for
  instance, are configured in code but not editable from the GUI, and fields whose value is at its
  default - a single cycle, an empty matrix - are hidden from the run panel rather than shown as
  clutter.</p>

<p>The two policy fields take a fixed set of values. <code>upload_test_package</code>:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Upload on every run. The safe choice while the suite is changing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>if_missing</code></td>
      <td>Upload only when the endpoint does not already have the package. The default.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Never upload - the endpoint is expected to have the package already.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code>, where <code>defer</code> is available to suites and groups only:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Update the endpoint application before every run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>on_mismatch</code></td>
      <td>Update only when the endpoint application does not match the controller. The default for a test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Leave the endpoint application as it is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>defer</code></td>
      <td>Express no opinion and leave the decision to the level below. The default for a suite or group.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
